  ],
  "text": "The sky is blue for exactly the same reason a sunset is red. Sunlight looks white, but it is every colour stacked on top of each other. Air molecules are tiny. Far too tiny to bounce a long red wave off. But a short blue wave is just the right size to be knocked sideways. So blue gets scattered all over the sky, and that is what reaches your eyes. At sunset the light arrives sideways, with far more air to cross. By the time it gets to you, every bit of blue has already been scattered away. So a red sky is not different light. It is the leftovers of the same light. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
